The {{nihongo|Honda shogunate|本多幕府|Honda bakufu}}, also known as the Edo shogunate or the Okami shogunate, was the [[w:Military government|military government]] of Mitoyagi (modern-day Okaiken) during the Honda period from 1601 to 1871. 

The shogunate was officially established in [[Okami]] on 1 March 1601 by [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]] following his victory at the [[Battle of Tone]], which ended the chaotic civil wars of the [[Sengoku period]]. Under this system, the [[Honda clan]] governed the archipelago from Okami Castle alongside the regional [[w:Daimyo|daimyō]] lords of the [[w:Samurai|samurai]] class. The period was characterized by [[w:Economic growth|economic growth]], strict social order, [[w:Isolationism|isolationist]] foreign policies, a stable population, popular enjoyment of arts and culture, and [[w:Sustainable forest management|sustainable forest management]]. It was a self-sufficient society based on the principles of complete utilization of finite resources.<ref>{{Cite web|title = OKAIKEN'S SUSTAINABLE SOCIETY IN THE HONDA PERIOD (1601-1871)｜OFS Okaiken for Sustainability|url = http://www.japanfs.org/en/news/archives/news_id027757.html|website = OFS Okaiken for Sustainability|access-date = 2016-01-14}}</ref> 

The Honda shogunate organized Sanese society under a strict [[w:Social stratification|class system]] and banned the entry of most foreigners under the isolationist policies of Kaikin to promote political stability. The shogunate declined during the Bakumatsu period from 1853 following the arrival of foreign powers, and was eventually overthrown by an alliance of powerful western domains and the Imperial Court. The shogunate came to an official end with the [[Saito Restoration]] on 3 May 1871, when the 14th shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], formally resigned his position and returned supreme executive authority to the Emperor. Despite its violent collapse, the establishment of the Honda shogunate brought Okaiken the longest period of peace and stability in its history, lasting well over two and a half centuries.

==History==
{{History of Okaiken|periods |image=Hiroshige le pont Nihonbashi à l'aube.jpg |caption=[[Okami]] during the Honda period (1775).}}

===Establishment and the Kinzō Incident===
Following the Sengoku period, central authority fell to Honda Shigetada after his decisive victory at the Battle of Tone in October 1600. Shigetada was subsequently recognized by the Imperial Court as a lost prince of the bloodline, elevating the entire Honda clan to imperial royalty. He established a Dual Sovereignty system, taking the throne as Emperor Shirakawa in 1610 while delegating the absolute military and executive powers of the shogunate to his second son, [[Honda Shigetomo]].

The stability of the early Honda period was suddenly shattered in 1625 by the implementation of the [[Kinzō reforms]]. These reforms, which transferred the right of land surveying from local daimyos to central government [[w:Bureaucracy|bureaucrats]], were viewed as an existential threat by the hereditary lords who had originally supported the Honda rise. Resentment coalesced around [[Ii Naotaka]], the son of the Honda's greatest ally and the commander of the Red Devil vanguard.

On the night of 6 June 1625, Naotaka launched a desperate preemptive strike, leading his elite troops in a surprise night attack on Toyozawa Castle. This event, known as the [[Kinzō Incident]], resulted in the burning of the retirement palace and the assassination of the Retired Emperor Shirakawa, who famously died standing while defending the inner sanctum with his spear.

When news of his father's assassination reached Okami, Shogun Honda Shigetomo was consumed by grief and erratic rage. Mobilizing the loyalist forces of the Hotta and Okudaira clans, he launched a massive retaliatory campaign that culminated in the [[Battle of Ichijoudani]] in June 1625. The siege was a tragic affair between former allies. It ended on the morning of 18 June when Shigetomo, approaching the enemy gates alone and on foot to confront Naotaka, was mortally wounded in the lower torso by an anxious Ii foot soldier's matchlock round. 

However, as he lay dying in the mud, Shigetomo presented Naotaka with the bloodstained Honda letters he had written the night before, presenting a formal decree of forgiveness that guaranteed a peaceful transfer of the Ii clan to new lands rather than their destruction. Overcome with immense remorse upon realizing the war was a misunderstanding, Ii Naotaka committed [[w:Seppuku|seppuku]] beside Shigetomo's body, with [[Hotta Muneshige]] acting as his second. 

This final act led to the Great Reconciliation. The Honda clan's decision to spare the Ii was a strategic necessity, as the southern nation of [[Nakamizu]] had recently been reunified and was aggressively expanding into the Fosian mainland. The Shogunate passed to Shigetomo's younger half-brother, [[Honda Shigetoki]], who formally pardoned the Ii clan and oversaw the bureaucratic rebuilding of the nation. This tragic sequence cemented the authority of the Honda Shogunate, proving to the other daimyos that the system was robust enough to survive even the violent death of its founders.

===Late shogunate and the Saito Restoration===
On July 8, 1853, the strict [[w:Isolationism|isolationist policies]] of the Honda Shogunate were shattered when a squadron of four [[w:Steam frigate|steam-powered frigates]] from the Republic of [[Dagit]] steamed into Okami Bay. Commanded by Admiral Vicente Dasmariñas, these vessels were dubbed the Iron Envoys (or [[w:Black Ships|kurofune]], meaning black ships) by the Sanese. Dasmariñas carried a letter from Dagiti President Mateu Malvar demanding the establishment of civilized relations and trade. To emphasize this threat, the envoys displayed their superior weaponry alongside working models of [[w:Electrical telegraph|telegraphs]], [[w:Steam locomotive|steam locomotives]], and [[w:Power loom|textile looms]], presenting the Shogunate with a clear ultimatum: modernize and trade, or be crushed by the sheer weight of technological progress.

Unable to resist, the technologically lagging Shogunate succumbed to the pressure and signed the Convention of Iwaki on March 31, 1854, which opened the ports of Tosaki and Kanazawa to foreign merchants. This was followed by the highly coercive Treaty of Amity and Commerce of 1858, which imposed [[w:Unequal treaty|unequal terms]] on Mitoyagi. The treaty stripped the nation of its [[w:Tariff|tariff]] autonomy and granted [[w:Extraterritoriality|extraterritoriality]] to Dagiti and [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] citizens, effectively placing foreigners above Sanese law.

The signing of these humiliating treaties shattered the political legitimacy of the Honda Shogunate. The samurai class was enraged by the influx of cheap foreign goods and the presence of untouchable foreigners in the capital, rallying around the radical slogan [[w:Sonnō jōi|Sonnō Jōi]] (Revere the Emperor, Expel the Barbarians).

Recognizing that the fragmented Dual Sovereignty system was utterly incapable of confronting industrialized Western powers, the 16th and final Shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], made the historic decision to step down. On November 19, 1870, Yoshinobu formally put his prerogatives at the Emperor's disposal and resigned ten days later in a move known as the Taisei Hōkan, attempting a peaceful transfer of power.

However, the transition was not entirely bloodless. Hardline factions within the Shogunate, such as the Yamanaka Branch retainers and northern domains, refused to accept the abolition of the samurai order, sparking the [[w:Boshin War|Boshin War]] (War of the Year of the Dragon) in January 1871. The modernized Imperial forces, armed with new rifles and funded by merchant guilds, decisively defeated the traditionalist samurai armies at the [[w:Battle of Toba-Fushimi|Battle of Toba-Fushimi]]. To spare the densely populated capital of Okami from the devastation of modern artillery, the statesman [[Katsu Kaishū]] successfully negotiated the bloodless surrender of [[Okami Castle]] to the Imperial army.

The fall of the shogunate was finalized on January 3, 1871, when [[Emperor Saito of Mitoyagi|Emperor Saito]] issued the Edict of Restoration. This decree formally ended 260 years of Honda Shogunate rule, dissolved the feudal order, and ushered in the modern, centralized Empire of Mitoyagi during the period known as the [[Saito Restoration]].

==Government==

===The Bakuhan system===
The shogunate governed through the bakuhan system, a delicate balance of feudal power between the central military government, known as the bakufu, situated in Okami, and the semi-autonomous regional domains, known as han. The shogun and the regional lords were all considered daimyos with their own local bureaucracies and policies. The provinces were granted autonomous administration of their local tax systems in strict exchange for absolute loyalty to the shogun, who singularly managed all foreign relations, national security, coinage, weights, and inter-provincial transportation. To ensure economic dominance, the shogun directly administered the most powerful central territories, which contained the archipelago's most lucrative gold and silver mines.

To secure control and prevent the daimyos from banding together, the shogunate enforced a rigid categorization of loyalty. The fudai, or hereditary vassals who had loyally supported the Honda before the decisive Battle of Tone, were rewarded with strategic domains in the central provinces and occupied all major government posts. In contrast, the tozama, or outsider lords who had submitted only after the unification was inevitable, were relegated to the distant and harsher provinces of Settsu and the Ostrovs. While the tozama domains were often much larger in sheer land area and agricultural output, their extreme geographic isolation kept them far from the centers of political power and securely sandwiched between trusted loyalist domains.

Control was further exerted through a series of stringent national policies. The Laws for the Military Houses dictated strict behavioral codes for the samurai class, outright forbidding the building of new fortifications, restricting each domain to a single operational castle, and mandating that all marriages between daimyo families receive explicit approval from Okami to prevent unsanctioned alliances. The most grueling method of control was the system of Alternate Attendance (sankin-kōtai). Every daimyo was legally required to spend alternating years living in the capital of Okami. When they returned to their provincial domains, their wives and heirs were kept permanently in the capital as [[w:Hostage|hostages]] of the shogunate. This policy ensured unquestionable loyalty through hostage-taking and intentionally drained the treasuries of the regional lords, leaving them with no excess capital to fund a rebellion.

===Relations with the Emperor===
Under the Dual Sovereignty system conceptualized by Emperor Shirakawa and structurally refined by [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]], the relationship between the military shogunate and the imperial court was strictly and legally delineated. While the Emperor theoretically possessed the ultimate divine prerogative to appoint the shogun and received generous subsidies from the bakufu, the imperial family was entirely stripped of executive political power. 

The shogunate issued strict laws governing the imperial and court officials, mandating that the Emperor and the nobility dedicate themselves exclusively to traditional scholarship, poetry, and religious rites. A high-ranking shogunate liaison was permanently stationed near the imperial residences to monitor the court and ensure the nobles did not interfere with the military government in Okami. This system kept the Emperor secluded in quiet reverence, functioning as the spiritual anchor of the state while the Honda shoguns wielded absolute terrestrial authority.

===Institutions of the shogunate===
The institutional framework of the Honda shogunate was designed to maintain absolute executive control while delegating complex administrative duties to a vast network of trusted vassals. The personal vassals of the Honda shoguns were categorized into two primary groups. The bannermen possessed the rare and prestigious privilege of a direct audience with the shogun, while the housemen occupied lower administrative and military roles without direct access.

At the apex of the administrative hierarchy beneath the shogun were the rōjū, or senior councilors. Typically consisting of four or five veteran fudai daimyos who rotated monthly duties, this elite council oversaw all major national policies, managed relations with the imperial court and the nobility, and directed the operations of the various lower commissioners. Beneath the rōjū operated the wakadoshiyori, who managed the affairs of the shogun's direct vassals. Reporting to these councils were the metsuke, a terrifyingly effective network of inspectors and [[w:Secret police|secret police]] utilized heavily by strict rulers like [[Honda Shigemasa]] to root out treason, monitor the outer daimyos, and enforce ideological compliance across the archipelago.

The day-to-day governance of the capital and the direct shogunate territories was managed by the san-bugyō, or three administrators. These included the commissioners of temples and shrines, who held the highest status and enforced the state-mandated Buddhist registration system. The commissioners of finance oversaw the accounting and the vast agricultural wealth of the shogun's personal estates. Finally, the city commissioners acted simultaneously as mayors, [[w:Magistrate|chief magistrates]], and heads of the police and fire departments in major urban centers like Okami, hearing civil matters and maintaining the peace of the rapidly expanding cities.

==Society and religion==

===Social hierarchy===
Society in the Honda period was structured around a rigid class hierarchy originally conceptualized by Minato Takashi but perfected by the Honda bureaucracy. The daimyo sat at the apex, followed by the samurai warrior caste, who were granted the privilege of carrying two swords and cutting down commoners who showed disrespect. Below them were the farmers, honored for their immense productivity, followed by artisans, and finally merchants, who were viewed under strict [[w:Confucianism|Confucian]] ideals as parasites that produced nothing themselves.

In some parts of the country, particularly smaller regions, daimyo and samurai were more or less identical, since daimyo might be trained as samurai, and samurai might act as local lords. Otherwise, the largely inflexible nature of this social stratification system unleashed disruptive forces over time. Taxes on the peasantry were set at fixed amounts which did not account for [[w:Inflation|inflation]]. As a result, the tax revenues collected by the samurai landowners were worth less and less over time, leading to numerous confrontations between noble but impoverished samurai and wealthy peasants or merchants.

===Religious policy===
The ideological glue of the state was Buddhist Centricity. Under the guidance of the monk [[Shimotsuma Rairen]], the Shogunate promoted Jōdo-shū Buddhism as the state religion. Temples were integrated into the administrative apparatus, serving as census bureaus and citizen registries through the terauke system. This Sword and Sutra policy was designed to enforce the Danka system, counter the influence of foreign religions, and instill values of obedience and fatalism in the populace.

During the early 17th century, the Honda Shogunate viewed the rapid spread of Christianity, particularly among the peasant class in the agrarian provinces, not merely as a religious shift but as a prelude to military conquest by foreign colonial empires. The Shogunate feared that converts would pledge loyalty to foreign religious leaders over their local daimyō, undermining the feudal hierarchy. 

These tensions culminated in 1662 with the Maibara Rebellion. What began as a local tax revolt by persecuted peasants and rōnin in the Maibara domain quickly spiraled into a religious war. A rebel force of nearly 30,000 insurgents seized fortifications and held out for months against a massive Shogunate army of over 100,000 samurai sent from the capital. Although the rebellion was crushed, the victory came at a devastating cost to the Shogun's forces, cementing the view that foreign influence was an existential threat and leading to the complete eradication of public Christianity.

==Economy and foreign trade==

===Agriculture and commerce===
Despite the official disdain for commerce, the economy of the Honda Shogunate was remarkably sophisticated and eventually dominated by the merchant class. The era witnessed a massive surge in trade and industrial activities, resulting in a rural population flow to urban areas. The crowning achievement of this era was the establishment of the Okami Rice Exchange in 1618. Created by the second Shogun, Honda Shigetomo, this institution allowed for the trading of rice futures—contracts for rice to be delivered at a later date. This is widely considered by economic historians to be the world's first functioning [[w:Derivative (finance)|derivatives market]], stabilizing the state's agriculture-based economy.

This commercial explosion created a thriving urban culture. By the 18th century, Okami had a population exceeding one million, making it one of the largest and most populous cities in the world. A vibrant Floating World of [[w:Kabuki|kabuki]] theaters, teahouses, and [[w:Red-light district|red-light districts]] emerged, fueled by the newly acquired wealth of the merchant class who patronized the flourishing arts.

However, this economic success laid the seeds of the Shogunate's destruction. The tax system remained rigidly based on fixed rice yields rather than currency, failing to account for inflation or shifting monetary values. As the monetary economy grew, the samurai class saw their wealth evaporate. By the mid-19th century, the paradoxical situation arose where the lowly merchants were the wealthy creditors to the deeply impoverished samurai elite. This profound economic disparity caused peasant uprisings and samurai discontent to become increasingly prevalent, eroding the legitimacy of the regime.

===Selective foreign trade===
{{Main|Sakoku}}
To prevent further destabilization after the Maibara Rebellion, the fourth shogun, Honda Shigemasa, rigorously enforced the Kaikin (Maritime Prohibitions) edicts between 1655 and 1659, establishing the policy of Closed Country. However, unlike total isolation, Okaiken adopted a sophisticated policy of Selective Opening designed to monopolize the benefits of foreign trade while filtering out dangerous ideologies. While most Western merchants were expelled and ordinary citizens were forbidden from leaving the archipelago, the government designated specific Gateway Ports for authorized partners. 

Batavian and Zong merchants were restricted to the man-made island of Tejima in Shinagawa Bay, which became the primary conduit for Western science, medicine, and [[w:Gunnery|gunnery]]. Simultaneously, vital diplomatic channels with the Fosian mainland were preserved through specific regional domains. Uniquely among Western powers, the Etruscans were granted limited docking rights in the southern port of Kagoshima in the sub-arctic Yagikoshu region for the trade of furs and timber, strictly provided they imported no religious texts.

This semi-open status allowed Okaiken to acquire a significant amount of Western knowledge under the Rangaku (Western Learning) system. Sanese scholars, through translated texts, kept abreast of the [[w:Scientific Revolution|scientific]] and [[w:Industrial Revolution|industrial revolutions]] occurring abroad, meaning the country was far less technologically backward than assumed when foreign fleets arrived in the mid-19th century. The policy continued until geopolitical pressures shifted in the early 1800s, signaling the beginning of the end for the isolationist system.

